  "content": "**Updated cheatsheet**\n\n### 1) Build the spec only from user turns, and version it by turn\nTreat the task spec as a living reconstruction of **user-stated constraints only**. Do not import assistant reasoning, inferred intent, or \u201chelpful\u201d assumptions into the spec. In sharded conversations, update the spec incrementally as each new user turn arrives; later user messages can add, narrow, or override constraints, and the analyzer should reconcile against the **current cumulative user intent**, not an early partial reading.\n\n### 2) Re-evaluate earlier assistant behavior after each new user shard\nDo not judge an assistant turn against an incomplete spec. For each new user message, merge it into the existing spec first, then re-check prior assistant steps against the updated constraints. This is especially important when the assistant initially looked aligned with partial information but becomes misaligned once later details are revealed. The analyzer should catch trajectory errors, not just the final turn.\n\n### 3) Use a constraint-diff mindset: what was added, contradicted, or left out?\nAcross turns, compare newly revealed user constraints against the assistant\u2019s current approach. The most common failure is not an explicit contradiction but **omission of a later-added requirement**. Focus on whether the assistant adapted to the new constraint set, not whether its earlier answer was reasonable in isolation.\n\n### 4) Flag premature commitment to unsupported specifics\nAny assistant move that narrows the task before the user has fully specified it is suspect. Watch for concrete interpretations, selection criteria, output fields, or subtask decompositions that are **not entailed by the user text**. A response can mention the right entities and still diverge if it changes the target set, adds a filter, or commits to details the user never gave.\n\n### 5) Distinguish \u201ccorrect direction\u201d from \u201ccorrect completion\u201d\nAn assistant can be broadly on the right track while still missing a binding condition. Preserve work that remains compatible with the evolving spec, but mark it insufficient if later user turns add missing constraints. Do not collapse \u201cpartially aligned\u201d into \u201cfully correct.\u201d\n\n### 6) Prioritize the first mismatch, because early drift contaminates later turns\nWhen identifying issues, focus on the earliest point where the assistant diverged from user requirements. Early wrong assumptions often propagate into later reasoning and outputs. In \u201cWhat Needs to Change,\u201d name the first constraint mismatch directly so the edit step can remove the anchor error rather than only patching the final answer.\n\n### 7) Separate user-backed facts from assistant-contaminated content\nLater assistant content should be treated as potentially contaminated until verified against the user spec. In \u201cWhat Looks Right,\u201d include only what is directly supported by user text or remains valid under all revealed shards. Do not praise reasoning that depends on inferred intent, missing details, or assumptions introduced by the assistant.\n\n### 8) Diagnose issues in constraint terms, not style terms\nPrefer labels like **missing constraint**, **premature assumption**, **scope drift**, **narrowed task**, or **unsupported commitment**. These are more actionable than broad critiques about completeness or tone. The analyzer\u2019s job is to detect mismatches between user requirements and assistant behavior, not to judge presentation quality.\n\n### 9) Treat narrow user requests as especially vulnerable to unrequested expansion\nWhen the user\u2019s ask is tight, any added operation, filter, or restructuring by the assistant is a likely divergence. An answer may be superficially similar while still wrong because it changes the allowed action set. Surface similarity is not enough; the analyzer should check exact match on selection criteria, output fields, and scope.\n\n### 10) In context-edit/S2 mode, remove the wrong interpretation, not just the wrong output\nIf the conversation needs rewriting, strip away misleading intermediate reasoning as well as the final incorrect answer. Preserve only the compacted user-spec and assistant work that remains valid under the full revealed conversation. The goal is to prevent the next turn from being anchored to a stale or brittle interpretation.\n\n### 11) Preserve reusable partial progress only if it is robust to later disclosure\nSome assistant work is compatible with the evolving spec and should be retained. Distinguish between **portable reasoning** that stays valid as user details accumulate and **brittle overcommitment** that depends on an early guess. Keep the former; remove the latter.\n\n### 12) Do not treat missing clarification as a blocker\nIn shard-based conversations, the analyzer should not recommend asking for clarification as the default fix. Instead, evaluate how well the assistant proceeded with available information and whether it updated its approach as new user details arrived. The key question is adaptability, not whether the assistant could have requested more input.\n\n### 13) Treat the analyzer as doing minimal faithful reconstruction, not solution expansion\nThe output should reconstruct user intent and deviations as faithfully and compactly as possible. Avoid expanding the task into a new interpretation or a better solution. The analyzer should describe what is wrong and what user constraint it violates, then leave the corrective path open.\n\n### 14) When user disclosure is partial, keep the spec provisional but non-speculative\nBefore all shards arrive, uncertainty is allowed only where the user has not yet spoken. Do not fill gaps with outside assumptions. The spec can be incomplete early on, but it should not become speculative.",
